facing pair of markers with a straight line of the marker colour. A row with a marker at both ends is filled completely, and so is a column with a marker at top and bottom. In pairs 2 and 3 the two lines cross, and the crossing cell is simply the marker colour.\n\n## Finding the marker colour without hard-coding it\n\nThe marker colour changes from pair to pair (2, 3, 8), so the program has to detect it. The defining property is that **every cell of that colour lies on the grid border**. The noise colours fail this test. Pair 1's colour 8 does touch the border at (12,0), but it also has interior cells, so it is rejected.\n\nTo guard against an unlucky case, I also require the colour to form at least one facing pair: two opposite ends of the same row, or the top and bottom of the same column.\n\n## Construction\n\n1. Start a blank canvas filled with the background colour. This is the \"erase all noise\" step.\n2. For each row whose first and last cells are both the marker colour, fill that row.\n3. For each column whose top and bottom cells are both the marker colour, fill that column.\n\nThe rows and columns use the same colour, so drawing order does not matter at crossings.\n\n## Test input\n\nIn the test, colour 6 appears at (0,3), (15,3), (5,0) and (5,18). All four cells are on the border, while colours 7 and 8 have interior cells. So 6 is the marker. The output is a blank 16×19 grid with row 5 filled with 6 and column 3 filled with 6, crossing at (5,3).","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    bg = background(g)\n    border = np.zeros_like(g, dtype=bool)\n    border[0, :] = border[-1, :] = border[:, 0] = border[:, -1] = True\n\n    marker = None\n    for c in np.unique(g):\n        if c == bg:\n            continue\n        m = g == c\n        if not np.all(m <= border):\n            continue\n        rows = [y for y in range(H) if g[y, 0] == c and g[y, W - 1] == c]\n        cols = [x for x in range(W) if g[0, x] == c and g[H - 1, x] == c]\n        if rows or cols:\n            marker = (c, rows, cols)\n            break\n\n    out = np.full_like(g, bg)\n    emit(out, \"Erase all scattered noise, leaving only the background.\")\n    if marker is None:\n        emit(out, \"No border marker pairs found.\")\n        return out\n    c, rows, cols = marker\n    for y in rows:\n        out[y, :] = c\n        emit(out, f\"Row {y} has marker colour {c} at both ends: connect them with a full horizontal line.\")\n    for x in cols:\n        out[:, x] = c\n        emit(out, f\"Column {x} has marker colour {c} at top and bottom: connect them with a full vertical line.\")\n    emit(out, \"Final grid: only the lines joining facing border markers remain.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"topology":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Pair 1's colour 8 does touch the border at (12,0), but it also has interior cells, so it is rejected."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"All four cells are on the border, while colours 7 and 8 have interior cells."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"689c2ff8f8df6d1b2b896d0292263435d9a7c5f5f2d32103c35cb0fd63b96da2","task_sha256":"fb62cc782ac8e506e675848be8ee35b530d43c30fc01a5e101261f5fe44afaf1","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
